\title{Product Manifolds & Subspace Contraction}

\subtree{
  \title{Section–Retraction Charts on Product Spaces}

  \p{
    Structured parameter spaces factor as products #{\mathcal{M} = \prod_{\ell=1}^L \mathcal{M}_\ell} across multiplicative scales, periodic phases, probability simplices, and discrete sets. Each factor #{\mathcal{M}_\ell} is equipped with a section #{\psi_\ell : \mathcal{M}_\ell \to \mathcal{U}_\ell \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_\ell}} into flat solver coordinates and a retraction #{\rho_\ell : \mathcal{U}_\ell \to \mathcal{M}_\ell} satisfying #{\rho_\ell \circ \psi_\ell = \mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{M}_\ell}}, so neighbor search and trust-region perturbations operate in #{\mathcal{U} = \prod_\ell \mathcal{U}_\ell} while evaluations retract cleanly onto #{\mathcal{M}}:
  }

  ##{
    \begin{CD}
      \mathcal{M}_\ell @>{\psi_\ell}>> \mathcal{U}_\ell \\
      @V{\mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{M}_\ell}}VV @VV{\rho_\ell}V \\
      \mathcal{M}_\ell @= \mathcal{M}_\ell
    \end{CD}
  }

  \p{
    Euclidean distance on #{\mathcal{U}_\ell} pulls back to intrinsic geometry on each factor:
  }

  \ul{
    \li{
      \strong{Multiplicative intervals} ([baux.Log](docs.html#baux.Log)): On #{[a, b] \subset \mathbb{R}_+}, the logarithmic section and exponential retraction
      ##{
        \psi(x) = \frac{\log x - \log a}{\log b - \log a} \in [0, 1], \qquad \rho(u) = a\left(\frac{b}{a}\right)^{\Pi_{[0,1]}(u)}
      }
      pull back Euclidean distance to the scale-invariant metric #{ds^2 = \tfrac{dx^2}{x^2}} up to #{\bigl(\log b - \log a\bigr)^{-2}}.
    }
    \li{
      \strong{Circular phases} ([baux.Periodic](docs.html#baux.Periodic)): On #{\lbrack a, b\rparen \cong S^1} of period #{P = b - a} with phase #{\phi(x) = \tfrac{2\pi(x - a)}{P}}, the unit-circumference embedding and #{\operatorname{atan2}} retraction
      ##{
        \psi(x) = \left(\frac{1}{2} + \frac{\cos\phi(x)}{2\pi},\; \frac{1}{2} + \frac{\sin\phi(x)}{2\pi}\right), \qquad d_{\mathcal{U}}\bigl(\psi(x), \psi(x')\bigr) = \frac{1}{\pi}\left|\sin\left(\frac{\phi(x) - \phi(x')}{2}\right)\right|
      }
      induce chordal distance on #{S^1} without boundary-wrap discontinuities.
    }
    \li{
      \strong{Probability simplices} ([baux.Simplex](docs.html#baux.Simplex)): On #{\Delta^{d-1}}, the positive-orthant square-root section and projective retraction
      ##{
        \psi(p) = \bigl(\sqrt{p_1}, \dots, \sqrt{p_d}\bigr) \in S_+^{d-1}, \qquad \rho(u) = \frac{\max(u, 0)^{\odot 2}}{\Vert\max(u, 0)\Vert_2^2}
      }
      pull back the ambient metric to #{\tfrac{1}{4}\,g_{\mathrm{FR}}} on #{\operatorname{int}(\Delta^{d-1})}, with squared chordal distance matching squared Hellinger distance #{\sum_{j=1}^d \bigl(\sqrt{p_j} - \sqrt{q_j}\bigr)^2}.
    }
    \li{\strong{Constant-norm discrete codebooks} ([baux.categorical](docs.html#baux.categorical)): Finite sets #{\{1, \dots, C\}} embed into #{\mathbb{R}^{d_c}} via constant-norm orthogonal or Rademacher codewords #{\psi(c)}, reducing nearest-codeword retraction #{\rho(u) = \arg\min_c \Vert u - \psi(c)\Vert_2} to maximum inner-product decoding #{\arg\max_c \langle u, \psi(c)\rangle}.}
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{Stiefel Signal-Subspace Contraction}

  \p{
    When evaluations return correlated multi-output vectors #{y = \theta + \eta \in \mathbb{R}^M} concentrated near a low-rank signal subspace, a loading matrix #{L \in \mathbb{R}^{M \times r}} (#{1 \le r < M}) orthonormalizes onto the Stiefel manifold #{V = L(L^\top L)^{-\frac{1}{2}} \in \mathrm{St}(r, M)} ([baux.Stiefel](docs.html#baux.Stiefel)) with orthogonal complement #{V_\perp \in \mathrm{St}(M-r, M)}. Under #{P \eqdef (V^\top,\, V_\perp^\top)^\top : \mathbb{R}^M \xrightarrow{\cong} \mathbb{R}^r \oplus \mathbb{R}^{M-r}}, subspace contraction leaves tangent coordinates #{z_\parallel = V^\top y} intact while shrinking normal coordinates #{z_\perp = V_\perp^\top y} (Stein [[Ste56]](https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/berkeley-symposium-on-mathematical-statistics-and-probability/Proceedings-of-the-Third-Berkeley-Symposium-on-Mathematical-Statistics-and/chapter/Inadmissibility-of-the-Usual-Estimator-for-the-Mean-of-a/bsmsp/1200501656), James & Stein [[JS61]](https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/berkeley-symposium-on-mathematical-statistics-and-probability/Proceedings-of-the-Fourth-Berkeley-Symposium-on-Mathematical-Statistics-and/chapter/Estimation-with-Quadratic-Loss/bsmsp/1200512173)):
  }

  ##{
    \begin{CD}
      \mathbb{R}^M @>{P}>> \mathbb{R}^r \oplus \mathbb{R}^{M-r} \\
      @V{\hat{T}_{\alpha}}VV @VV{I_r \oplus \alpha I_{M-r}}V \\
      \mathbb{R}^M @<<{P^\top}< \mathbb{R}^r \oplus \mathbb{R}^{M-r}
    \end{CD}
  }

  \p{
    With zero-mean noise variances #{v_\parallel \eqdef \mathbb{E}\Vert V^\top \eta\Vert_2^2} and #{v_\perp \eqdef \mathbb{E}\Vert V_\perp^\top \eta\Vert_2^2 > 0} and off-subspace signal energy #{b_\perp^2 \eqdef \Vert V_\perp^\top \theta\Vert_2^2}, the linear class #{\hat{\theta}_\alpha = V z_\parallel + \alpha V_\perp z_\perp} attains its minimum quadratic risk #{\mathcal{R}(\alpha) = \mathbb{E}\Vert\hat{\theta}_\alpha - \theta\Vert_2^2} at the oracle contraction factor #{\alpha^*}, strictly improving upon unshrunk estimation:
  }

  ##{
    \alpha^* = \frac{b_\perp^2}{b_\perp^2 + v_\perp}, \qquad \mathcal{R}(\alpha^*) = v_\parallel + \frac{v_\perp b_\perp^2}{b_\perp^2 + v_\perp} < v_\parallel + v_\perp = \mathcal{R}(1).
  }

  \p{
    Under isotropic Gaussian noise in normal codimension #{M - r \ge 3}, positive-part James–Stein shrinkage uniformly dominates #{\alpha = 1} ([[JS61]](https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/berkeley-symposium-on-mathematical-statistics-and-probability/Proceedings-of-the-Fourth-Berkeley-Symposium-on-Mathematical-Statistics-and/chapter/Estimation-with-Quadratic-Loss/bsmsp/1200512173), Efron & Morris [[EM73]](https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1973.10481350)), whereas [baux.stein](docs.html#baux.stein) applies the empirical moment-ratio plug-in across any #{M - r \ge 1}:
  }

  ##{
    \hat{\alpha} = \frac{\Vert z_\perp\Vert_2^2}{\Vert z_\perp\Vert_2^2 + \hat{v}_\perp}.
  }

  \p{
    When held-out calibration pairs are available, [baux.calibrate](docs.html#baux.calibrate) rescales predictive dispersion via affine weighted least squares and standardized residual quantiles, yielding finite-sample coverage on exchangeable splits and an empirical dispersion adjustment on adaptive optimization histories where sequential selection breaks exchangeability.
  }

  \p{
    Below, a PyTree product domain combining logarithmic gain, periodic phase, simplex filter weights, and categorical windowing drives a noisy 6-channel spectral response concentrated near a rank-2 Stiefel frame:
  }

  \pre{
import math
import numpy as np
import baux

rng = np.random.default_rng(3)
space = {
    "gain": baux.Log(1e-3, 1.0),
    "phase": baux.Periodic(0.0, 2.0 * math.pi),
    "weights": baux.Simplex(4),
    "window": baux.categorical(["hann", "hamming", "blackman", "kaiser"]),
}
loadings = np.array([
    [1.0, 0.0],
    [0.8, 0.2],
    [0.2, 0.9],
    [-0.4, 0.7],
    [0.5, -0.5],
    [-0.3, -0.6],
])
frame = baux.Stiefel(loadings)
target_w = np.array([0.45, 0.30, 0.15, 0.10])
win_loss = {"hann": 0.0, "hamming": 0.04, "blackman": 0.08, "kaiser": 0.01}


def spectral_channels(p):
    g_err = math.log10(p["gain"] / 0.05)
    ph_err = math.sin(0.5 * (p["phase"] - 1.2))
    w_err = float(np.sum((np.sqrt(p["weights"]) - np.sqrt(target_w)) ** 2))
    latent = np.array([-(g_err**2 + w_err + win_loss[p["window"]]), -(ph_err**2)])
    return loadings @ latent + 0.15 * rng.normal(size=6)


pipe = (
    baux.enn(k=8, geometry="conformal")
    | baux.stein(frame)
    | baux.ucb(beta=1.5)
    | baux.regions(count=3)
)
state = baux.init(space, pipe, key=baux.key(19))
for _ in range(10):
    state, batch = baux.ask(state, num_arms=3)
    y = np.vstack([spectral_channels(p) for p in batch.params])
    state, mu_shrunk = baux.tell(state, batch, y)

assert mu_shrunk.shape == (3, 6) and state.y_obs.shape == (30, 6)
  }
}
